I read it closely. The memoir trick is a genuine invention, the section on discarded designs is the most convincing methods writing in the draft, and the French–German split on the Depression is the kind of finding people will retell at dinner. Now the front-stabbing, roughly in order of how much each problem threatens the paper.

**Your witness fails on the questions you need it most, and one of your three headline results collapses because of it.** Look at your own Table 1. On the Tsar's murder, the witness — the 1929 reader, for whom the execution at Yekaterinburg was world news a decade old — puts 2% on the truth. Two percent. That question is broken, probably in the wording of the endings, and the gap of +2.71 computed from it is a ratio of two tiny, unstable numbers. The same disease infects "which houses failed first" (witness 13%) and "who ruled Russia" (witness 4%). Your stated rule — a gap means something only when the witness is confident — is right, but the table violates it and the abstract quotes the Tsar as a finding. You need a hard rule applied without mercy: a question enters the paper only if the witness passes it, say 50% on the truth. By that rule the "unforeseen" column shrinks to the republic and the Rentenmark. Both survive beautifully. The Tsar goes, and better it goes now than in a referee report.

**Ten answered questions is not an empirical base, and the ten were not drawn from anywhere.** The meter declined 16 of 26, and the 26 themselves were written by you. A skeptic's one-line rejection writes itself: the authors chose the questions, so of course most catastrophes look thinkable. The fix is to define the universe of events before writing a single question — you're aiming at Barro, so take the Barro–Ursúa disaster list for your window, plus a fixed rule for adding political catastrophes, generate questions for every event by a written recipe, pre-register the set, and report every decline. Ten hand-picked gaps is a beautiful pilot. It is not yet evidence.

**"Foreseen" has no control group.** A gap of +0.2 on Russian debts — small compared to what? You never show the gap distribution for ordinary, undramatic outcomes: election results, treaty locations, technology adoptions that arrived on schedule. Without that yardstick, nobody knows whether +0.5 is foresight or noise. Run the same instrument on a batch of mundane between-cutoff outcomes and report the distribution. Then "the war's spread was as foreseeable as the location of a peace conference" becomes a sentence with content.

**Your support claim is measured with the wrong instrument, and the right one is hiding in your appendix.** When you hand the 1913 reader four endings including "President Ebert," you have put the republic into its head yourself. Multiple choice can measure weighting; it cannot measure whether an outcome was thinkable, because the researcher does the thinking by writing the option. The thing that measures thinkability is free generation — does the reader ever *volunteer* the outcome? — and you already have it in Appendix C: the 1913 reader volunteers the Tsar's deposition in two-thirds of completions and no reader ever volunteers the corporal. That is your support evidence. Restructure the paper on that division: generation measures what was in the picture, betting slips measure how the picture was weighted. It makes the Barro section twice as sharp, because the two halves of his assumption now each have their own instrument.

**The availability heuristic and a token counter are observationally equivalent, and you haven't separated them.** Your central behavioral claim is that the pre-event mind weights the most recent precedent. But a language model trained on any corpus overweights what the corpus says most often, and the 1929 corpus contains far more text about the Munich putsch than about constitutional intrigue, more about the Federal Reserve's promise than its limits. Is the model exhibiting the psychology of an age, or arithmetic on page counts? You can test this: measure the print share of each precedent in the corpus and ask whether the model's bets exceed what raw frequency predicts. If they do, you have diagnostic expectations in the wild and the Bordalo–Gennaioli–Shleifer crowd will carry you around the room. If they don't — that's still a paper, about how the written record weighs the future, but it's a different paper and Section 7 must be rewritten. Right now you're claiming the stronger result on evidence consistent with the weaker one.

**Everything you measure is conditional, and Section 7 quietly pretends otherwise.** The memoir prompt tells the guesser the catastrophe happened — "the war was lost, who rules?" So you measure the shape of disasters given disaster, never the probability of disaster. Barro's calibration and Ferguson's bond puzzle are about the unconditional probability. Your logic still goes through — if investors could picture the war's shape, then quiet spreads mean they assigned it low probability, which is your weighting story — but that step deserves one careful, explicit paragraph, or a finance referee will write it for you, unkindly.

**The biggest single improvement costs you nothing but patience: the later witnesses.** Section 5 is the section everyone will want — the Depression and Hitler are the two disasters the entire rare-events literature is calibrated on — and it's the one section with no measurement, only a memoir read against the record. You have later cutoffs in the pipeline. When the 1939 or 1946 reader exists, Table 2 becomes gaps, the paper's best question gets a real answer, and the conclusion's "a reader trained to 1938 would let us" stops being an apology. I would seriously consider holding the flagship submission for that model. A v5 with hand-picked questions ships in the fall and gets remembered as a clever pilot; a v6 with the disaster-list universe and the 1939 witness is the definitive paper on this question, possibly for decades, because nobody else has the models.

Smaller but real: put error bars on every gap by bootstrapping over ending paraphrases, and if at all affordable, a second training seed for one cutoff so "the 1913 reader believes" has a standard error at the model level, not just the prompt level. Fix German certification — you diagnose it as answer wording, which is fixable — because your best color result, the French press remembering recoveries while the German press remembered collapse, currently rests on two uncertified instruments. And either explain or cut the modern reader's numbers in Table 5: a reader who knows Hitler was appointed legally scoring 33% on that question tells the referee your forced-choice arm can't read knowledge even when it's there, and you don't want that thought loose in their head while they judge the main arm.

Last, the scope question you actually asked. As written, this is a general-science paper: one new instrument, a handful of vivid findings, gorgeous prose. To make it the QJE paper, it needs the disaster-list universe, the witness-confidence rule, the two-instrument architecture, the frequency control, and ideally the 1939 reader. My advice is to stop thinking of that as one paper's revision list and decide which paper you're writing — and if the answer is both, the split is natural: the instrument, the exam, and the taxonomy of failed designs for the science venue; the disasters, the weighting bias, and Barro for economics. What I would not do is send v4 to a top-5 journal, because every weakness above is visible from the tables alone, and you only get one first impression with an idea this good.